    "summary": "High‑energy set with smoking Other One > Wharf Rat and stellar Pigpen moments",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a tight first set highlighted by Sugaree, Mr. Charlie, Jack Straw and Run Rudolph Run, then launches into an expansive second half anchored by a soaring Other One suite that flows seamlessly into Wharf Rat. Jerry’s vocals and guitar are superb, Phil’s bass drives the jams, and Pigpen adds soulful organ and vocal flair. Excellent soundboard sources make this a must‑listen for late‑1971 fans.",
    "review": "December 14, 1971 at Hill Auditorium finds the Dead firing on all cylinders as they close out a tumultuous year. The first set rolls out with crisp, swinging rhythm work – Truckin' opens the night and gives way to sweetly rendered Sugaree and a gritty Mr. Charlie, while Jack Straw and Run Rudolph Run showcase Pigpen’s unmistakable vocal grit. By the second set the band is in full flight: Cryptical Envelopment segues into a tight drum break that launches an epic Other One suite, its angular bass runs and Jerry’s explosive solo building into a symphonic transition onto Wharf Rat. The momentum carries through to a bright Sugar Magnolia before the night winds down with classic sing‑along codas. Across all four Betty Cantor soundboard releases the mix is remarkably clear – Bill Kreutzmann’s drums cut through like a snare‑tight pulse, Keith’s piano adds subtle color and Pigpen’s organ/harp textures sit perfectly in the blend. For anyone hunting a vivid snapshot of the Dead’s late‑1971 spell, this performance captures both the raw power and the nuanced interplay that defined the era.",
